Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This specification defines two classes of product: an installer, which installs the plugin and sets Claude Code to write its raw API bodies [CLAUDE-CODE] where the reader of them, raw_api_bodies.mtsv, reads them; and the plugin, whose hooks convert the raw API bodies after each response and name the file of sheets at the start of each session.
conformance.2	An installer conforms if it does what the prompt, directory, plugin and capture rules say, in that order, and nothing else. The plugin conforms if it holds what the hook rules say, with the manifest's name and its metadata fields, description, author and license [CLAUDE-CODE], and nothing else.
conformance.3	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.4	The Rules are normative. The first part of a rule's Id names what it governs: prompt the question asked, directory the data directory, plugin the plugin's installation, capture the capture setting, hook the plugin's hooks. A rule names each source it follows in brackets, as [CLAUDE-CODE]; the References list them, each marked normative or informative. This specification has no optional features: where a source leaves a choice to the implementation, a rule closes it, and where no source makes a choice, the rule says so.
conformance.5	Raw API bodies, index file, settings file, hook, plugin, marketplace and userConfig are as defined in [CLAUDE-CODE]; base directory as in [XDG-BASEDIR].
Rules
Id	Rule
prompt.1	Before changing anything, an installer MUST state each change it will make, and ask whether to proceed, No by default, acting only on y; with no terminal to ask on, it MUST refuse, as --interactive asks in cp, ln, mv and rm [GNU].
directory.1	The data directory is living-memory in the user's base directory for data files: on Linux $XDG_DATA_HOME, or ~/.local/share when it is unset, empty or not absolute [XDG-BASEDIR]; on macOS ~/Library/Application Support [APPLE-FS]. On any other system an installer MUST refuse, no source for its data directory being held. living-memory is the application's name [PLATFORMDIRS].
directory.2	In the data directory, the raw API bodies are the directory anthropic/claude_code/raw_api_bodies, the path of the module that reads them, and their MTSV file raw_api_bodies.mtsv beside it, the command's output name; the sources leave these names to the implementer [XDG-BASEDIR] [CLAUDE-CODE], and they are named after the sources' terms. An installer MUST create the directories that do not exist, with permission 0700 [XDG-BASEDIR], and an empty index file, index.jsonl, in raw_api_bodies when it has none, Claude Code appending a line to it for each successful response [CLAUDE-CODE], so that the directory is read as Claude Code's raw API bodies before its first call is recorded.
plugin.1	An installer MUST add the marketplace, with claude plugin marketplace add demos-ra/living-memory, and then install the plugin, with claude plugin install claude-code@living-memory --config data_dir=<the data directory>, which installs to user scope without an interactive step [CLAUDE-CODE]. If either fails, it MUST stop, changing nothing more.
capture.1	Last, an installer MUST set OTEL_LOG_RAW_API_BODIES to file:<the data directory>/anthropic/claude_code/raw_api_bodies under env in the user settings file, ~/.claude/settings.json, where Claude Code honors it [CLAUDE-CODE]: the file read as strict JSON [CLAUDE-CODE], an object holding only env when it does not exist, the variable added or replaced and every other member kept, and the file written beside its name and renamed onto it [POSIX-XSH-RENAME]. Setting it consents to everything the raw API bodies reveal [CLAUDE-CODE].
hook.1	The plugin, claude-code, MUST declare the userConfig option data_dir, of type directory and required, which the installer sets [CLAUDE-CODE]; and no version, so that it is versioned by the git commit of its source [CLAUDE-CODE].
hook.2	Stop runs when Claude has finished responding, and not when a user interrupts it [CLAUDE-CODE], so the calls of an interrupted turn are converted at the next Stop, the file being written whole each time. Its Stop hook MUST run, in exec form and async, living-memory ${user_config.data_dir}/anthropic/claude_code/raw_api_bodies, the option substituted in exec form only [CLAUDE-CODE], the MTSV file taking the command's output name. The command writes the whole file at each conversion, an MTSV file being an ordered sequence of sheets and a sheet an ordered sequence of records [MTSV-DRAFT], beside its name, and renames it onto it, so that two sessions converting at once each replace it whole [POSIX-XSH-RENAME].
hook.3	Its SessionStart hook MUST run, in exec form, echo with one argument, "Claude Code's past conversations are kept as MTSV in ${user_config.data_dir}/anthropic/claude_code/raw_api_bodies.mtsv, written after each response.": the plain-text stdout of a SessionStart hook is added as context that Claude can see, and such context is written as a statement of fact [CLAUDE-CODE], true before the first response is written as after.
